WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.067
Masked language modeling gives us a direct demonstration of BERT-style pretraining.

00:00:05.067 --> 00:00:10.677
We place the model’s mask token inside the sentence “The student submitted the mask.” The fill-mask pipeline

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:19.097
evaluates likely vocabulary tokens for the missing position and returns ranked predictions with scores.

00:00:19.097 --> 00:00:22.727
The model is not generating an unrestricted paragraph.

00:00:22.727 --> 00:00:27.612
It is predicting the missing token using context from both the left and the right.

00:00:27.612 --> 00:00:33.832
That bidirectional context is one reason encoder models work well for understanding tasks.

00:00:33.832 --> 00:00:40.712
Try replacing the sentence with one that has an ambiguous missing word and inspect the top five predictions.

00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:46.507
The results also remind us that language models learn statistical patterns from training data.

00:00:46.507 --> 00:00:53.027
A high-probability token is not guaranteed to be factually correct or appropriate for every domain.

00:00:53.027 --> 00:00:57.227
The demonstration is useful for understanding the pretraining objective,

00:00:57.227 --> 00:01:00.069
not for treating BERT as a general chat model.
